 <exercise xml:id="exercise-invertibility-gauss-jordan">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the matrix
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \\
          5 \amp 3
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Determine whether <m>A</m> is invertible. If it is, find <m>A^{-1}</m>
      using row reduction.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Form the augmented matrix <m>[A \mid I]</m> and row reduce.
      If the left side reduces to the identity matrix, then <m>A</m> is invertible.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The matrix <m>A</m> is invertible, and
      <m>
        A^{-1} =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          3 \amp -1 \\
          -5 \amp 2
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We begin with the augmented matrix
      <m>
        \left[
        \begin{array}{cc|cc}
          2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
          5 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp 1
        \end{array}
        \right].
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      After performing row operations to reduce the left side to the identity,
      we obtain
      <m>
        \left[
        \begin{array}{cc|cc}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp -5 \amp 2
        \end{array}
        \right].
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since the left side is the identity matrix, <m>A</m> is invertible,
      and the right side is <m>A^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-invertibility-solving-system">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        A =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp -2 \\
          2 \amp -3
        \end{bmatrix},
        \quad
        \mathbf{b} =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \\
          7
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol> 
        <li> Show that <m>A</m> is invertible. 
        </li>
       <li> Solve the equation <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> using <m>A^{-1}</m>.
       </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      First find <m>A^{-1}</m>. Then multiply both sides of
      <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> on the left by <m>A^{-1}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The matrix <m>A</m> is invertible, and the solution is
      <m>
        \mathbf{x} =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \\
          -1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The inverse of <m>A</m> is
      <m>
        A^{-1} =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          -3 \amp 2 \\
          -2 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      We compute
      <m>
        \mathbf{x}
        = A^{-1}\mathbf{b}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          -3 \amp 2 \\
          -2 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}
          4 \\
          7
        \end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \\
          -1
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-invertibility-conceptual">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>A</m> is a square matrix and there exists a vector
      <m>\mathbf{b}</m> such that the equation <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m>
      has more than one solution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Explain why <m>A</m> is not invertible.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Consider what invertibility would imply about the number of solutions
      to <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      If <m>A</m> were invertible, then multiplying both sides of
      <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> by <m>A^{-1}</m> would yield
      <m>\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\mathbf{b}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This equation would produce a unique solution.
      Since the system has more than one solution, this contradicts
      the existence of an inverse.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, <m>A</m> is not invertible.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      If <m>A</m> were invertible, then multiplying both sides of
      <m>A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}</m> by <m>A^{-1}</m> would yield
      <m>\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\mathbf{b}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This equation would produce a unique solution.
      Since the system has more than one solution, this contradicts
      the existence of an inverse.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, <m>A</m> is not invertible.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-bases-coordinates">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        \mathcal{B} =
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix},
        \begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\end{bmatrix}
        \right\}
      </m>
      be a basis of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Find the coordinate vector of
      <m>
        \mathbf{v} =
        \begin{bmatrix}3\\0\end{bmatrix}
      </m>
      relative to the basis <m>\mathcal{B}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Write <m>\mathbf{v}</m> as a linear combination of the basis vectors
      and solve for the coefficients.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
        [\mathbf{v}]_{\mathcal{B}} =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We seek scalars <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> such that
      <m>
        c_1
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
        +
        c_2
        \begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}3\\0\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      This gives the system
      <m>
        \begin{cases}
          c_1 + 2c_2 = 3 \\
          c_1 - c_2 = 0
        \end{cases}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solving, we find <m>c_1=1</m> and <m>c_2=1</m>.
      Therefore,
      <m>
        [\mathbf{v}]_{\mathcal{B}} =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-bases-uniqueness">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathcal{B}</m> be a basis of a vector space <m>V</m>.
      Suppose that a vector <m>\mathbf{v}\in V</m> satisfies
      <m>
        [\mathbf{v}]_{\mathcal{B}} =
        \begin{bmatrix}4\\-1\\2\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Explain why <m>\mathbf{v}</m> cannot have a different coordinate
      vector relative to <m>\mathcal{B}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Recall the definition of a basis and what it implies about linear
      combinations.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The coordinate vector of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> relative to
      <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is unique.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a basis, it is linearly independent.
      This means that there is exactly one way to express <m>\mathbf{v}</m>
      as a linear combination of the vectors in <m>\mathcal{B}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Coordinates record the coefficients in this linear combination.
      If there were two different coordinate vectors for <m>\mathbf{v}</m>,
      then <m>\mathbf{v}</m> would have two distinct representations as
      a linear combination of the basis vectors.
    </p>
    <p>
      This would contradict linear independence.
      Therefore, the coordinate vector is unique.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-change-of-basis">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>
        \mathcal{B} =
        \left\{
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix},
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
        \right\}
      </m>
      be a basis of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The coordinate vector of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> relative to
      <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is
      <m>
        \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{B}} =
        \begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\end{bmatrix}
      </m>.
      </p>
    <p>
      Find <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in standard coordinates.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Use the definition of coordinate vectors to reconstruct
      the actual vector.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>
        \mathbf{v} =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      By definition,
      <m>
        \mathbf{v}
        = 2
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}
        - 1
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>
        \mathbf{v}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}2\\0\end{bmatrix}
        -
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

</exercises>

</section>
